<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Organisation armée secrète</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1961">1961</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Secret Army Organisation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Organisation armée secrète (OAS) was a clandestine French far-right paramilitary group formed in early 1961 by army officers and European settlers opposed to Algerian independence. Through bombings and assassinations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> it tried to wreck the negotiations between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> and the Algerian nationalists, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evian-accords">Évian Accords</a> of 1962 it waged a scorched-earth campaign before most settlers left. It also tried to kill de Gaulle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/organisation-armee-secrete.jpg" alt="Organisation armée secrète" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alejandro Basombrio · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Embl%C3%A8me_de_l%27OAS.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Organisation armée secrète (OAS, Secret Army Organisation) was a far-right dissident French paramilitary organisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algerian-war">Algerian War</a>, founded in 1961 by Raoul Salan, Pierre Lagaillarde and Jean-Jacques Susini. It was officially formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in Francoist Spain, in January 1961, in response to the referendum of 8 January 1961 on self-determination for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> organised by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>. It drew together existing anti-FLN networks that called themselves counter-terrorists, self-defence groups or resistance. Its motto was L'Algérie est française et le restera, meaning Algeria is French and will remain so.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The OAS was anti-communist and authoritarian, like many colonists, yet it also included ex-communists, Algerian Jews and former members of the French Resistance as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> collaborators. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> it recruited mainly among openly fascist groups. By bombings and assassinations in metropolitan France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> between April 1961 and April 1962 it is estimated to have caused 2,000 deaths. Its first victim was the lawyer Pierre Popie. Roger Gavoury, the head of the police in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, was killed on 31 May 1961. The OAS became notorious for stroungas, attacks using plastic explosives.</p><p>After the Évian agreements of 19 March 1962 the OAS tried to prove that the FLN was restarting the war, setting off on average 120 bombs a day in March. On 26 March troops fired on settler demonstrators in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, killing 54 in the gunfight of the Rue d'Isly. Raoul Salan was captured in April 1962, and the OAS began a ceasefire on 17 June. Its attempts on de Gaulle included the ambush of 22 August 1962 at Petit-Clamart, planned by Jean Bastien-Thiry, who was not a member and was executed in March 1963.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>By 1963 the main OAS operatives were dead, in exile or in prison, and with the arrest of Gilles Buscia in 1965 the organisation effectively ceased to exist. Jailed members were amnestied under a law of July 1968, and generals still alive in November 1982, among them Salan and Edmond Jouhaud, were reintegrated into the army by another amnesty law.</p><p>Many members later took part in anti-communist struggles elsewhere. The chaplain Georges Grasset organised a flight route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. The Charles Martel Group, active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the 1970s and 1980s, was formed by OAS veterans. The organisation also left its mark on fiction, notably Frederick Forsyth's novel The Day of the Jackal (1971), in which it hires an assassin to kill de Gaulle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Organisation armée secrète” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Settler and army anger at Paris produced the crisis of May 1958, which returned Charles de Gaulle to power; he moved towards accepting self-determination, announced in September 1959, and survived the Generals’ Putsch of April 1961 and the terrorism of the settler <b>Secret Army Organisation</b> (OAS).</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
